+**Do not hand-maintain prices here.** The `agent-companion` plugin's `config/model-tiers.json` is the canonical, live-sourced routing table (asOf date, source URL, and minimum Claude Code version recorded per alias), and `docs/ROUTING.md` in that plugin is generated from it — run `node scripts/routing-table.mjs` there, or `node scripts/recommend.mjs --type <task-type>`, rather than reading numbers off this page. This section previously carried its own price table (reviewed 2026-08-28); it went stale exactly the way the plugin's own README warns a hardcoded copy will, and is replaced with a pointer plus the qualitative facts worth keeping in a page read at session start:
 
-- **`fable` is an exception, not a tier.** It sits in the same alias namespace as `opus`/`sonnet`/`haiku`, so it is one word away at every spawn, but it belongs to no weight on the scale. Spawning it requires a stated warrant naming the weight and why a cheaper tier cannot do the job. This is enforced, not merely requested - see the `agent-companion` plugin.
+- **`haiku` retires no sooner than 2026-10-15**, with no successor announced as of 2026-09-23 — the plugin's routing table stages a fallback (`sonnet` at `low` effort) automatically; nothing to do here unless a new Haiku ships, in which case update the plugin's config, not this file.
+- **`opus` is currently ~2x `sonnet`** on list price (was ~2.5x under the previous generation) — the gap narrowed, it did not widen; don't assume an old "2.5x" ratio when reasoning about cost tradeoffs.
+- **`fable` is currently ~2.5x `opus`** on list price (was ~2x) — the gap WIDENED, which strengthens the warrant bar below: prefer trying a higher-effort `opus` brief first (Anthropic's own escalation criterion is "at higher effort still falls short," not "this looks hard").
+- **`fable` is an exception, not a tier.** It sits in the same alias namespace as `opus`/`sonnet`/`haiku`, so it is one word away at every spawn, but it belongs to no weight on the scale. Spawning it requires a stated warrant naming the weight and why a cheaper tier cannot do the job — specifically, per the plugin's routing table, that an `opus` brief at higher effort was tried and fell short. This is enforced, not merely requested - see the `agent-companion` plugin.
 - **THE DEFAULT IS THE DANGER: a subagent with no `model` inherits the LEAD's model.** Nothing needs to choose a premium tier for a premium tier to be used. Observed 2026-08-28: four `fable` agents ran on a task that warranted none - no rule was broken, because no choice was ever made. Always set `model` explicitly on a spawn; an omitted model is a decision to pay the lead's rate.
-- **Pin by alias, never by version - but `fable` vs `opus` is an ALIAS difference, not a version one.** Within a family, pinning an older version saves nothing and adds deprecation risk. Across families it is a real 2x lever, so "version is a non-lever" does NOT license reaching for `fable`.
-- **Cost levers are tier + effort + prompt caching.** On Opus, output bills 5x input, and `effort` scales ALL output (thinking + answer + tool calls). `high` is the sweet spot, `xhigh` the recommended start for agentic work on Fable 5 / Opus 5 / Sonnet 5 (and Claude Code's own default), and `max` adds significant cost for relatively small quality gains - reserve it for genuinely frontier problems. Use `low` for subagents doing simple work.
+- **Pin by alias, never by version - but `fable` vs `opus` is an ALIAS difference, not a version one.** Within a family, pinning an older version saves nothing and adds deprecation risk. Across families it is a real cost lever (see the plugin's table for the current ratio), so "version is a non-lever" does NOT license reaching for `fable`.
+- **Cost levers are tier + effort + prompt caching.** `effort` scales ALL output (thinking + answer + tool calls) — see the plugin's per-alias `resolvesTo.defaultEffort` and effort-support list for what each current model actually accepts (older pinned versions differ: e.g. Opus 4.6 and Sonnet 4.6 have no `xhigh`). `max` adds significant cost for relatively small quality gains - reserve it for genuinely frontier problems. Use `low` for subagents doing simple work.
 - **Delegation is cheap only if the WORKER is cheap.** Every fresh subagent spawn pays a cold cache write; subagents were measured at 38.9% of a 30-day spend. The old line "over-delegation just wastes a haiku agent" holds ONLY when the agent is actually haiku. Under model inheritance, over-delegation is expensive - which is why the explicit-model rule above matters more than the delegate-everything rule.
